Actually, skill is not needed for firearms.  I have used firearms in all
of my slash'em ascensions to deal with Demogorgon and Vecna as well as
with most demon lords.  They are better than artifacts as long as you
have a sufficient number of well-enchanted (+4 or higher) bullets. (And
the bullets seem to hit whether you are skilled or not.) Firearms are
unbalanced in the sense that they are extremely powerful in the hands of
the player but rather harmless in the hands of monsters.  I believe, I
was never hit by a bullet, simply because my AC was too good when
firearms first turned up.  On the other hand, I think they are useful to
keep the game winnable for non-spellcasters, as melee is not a good idea
againts some foes...


> So no, I don't think SLASH'EM is unbalanced at all.  Maybe SLASH was,
> but even then, I liked to play it.

I think it is not much more unbalanced than nethack.  It is more
difficult (in terms of ascension rate - mine is about 1 in 4 characters
in nethack and about 1 in 7 in slashem), because the initial game is
more difficult, but once you have a character going, it is easier than
nethack (and less boring), as you depend much less on the RNG - most
necessary items/properties can be achieved in a systematic way. For
example, to get magic protection before the castle when your quest
artifact does not provide it can be a pain in the neck in nethack, as
cloaks of magic resistance are rare and gray dragons that leave scales
tend to be rare as well.  In slash'em you can either get the cursed -9
gray dragon scales on the lawful quest (requires a scroll of genocide or
a throne genocide) and upgrade it (via a wand of cancellation or a
shopkeeper) or you kill one-eyed Sam to get his gray dragon scale mail.
 Both difficult ways but doable and useful, if no wish is available and
no gray dragons leave scales.  Alternatively, you can use potions of
gain level to upgrade elven cloaks until one of them turns into a cloak
of magic resistance. If you are   lacking a crucial item, chances are it
can be found on the spiders level or in Sam's shop.  There is a
significant chance to get an amulet of flying in the lawful quest, there
are potions of enlightenment in Grund's stronghold, potions of
invulnerability in the spider level. And so on.  I like it when success
in a game depends more on your skillfulness and resources than on random
numbers. Arguably, in nethack this is true at a certain level as well.
But the waiting time is longer until the RNG has provided you with
everything that makes skill and faultless play the only issues.
